hy not just take it to the  next level the state of college right  now you could trace a lot back the  problems back through K through 12  education and just what a dismal state  that's in do you think that the solution  for that is just as simple as allowing  more choice oh absolutely and you know  it is it's actually odd how our current  system of public education has there  should you so many rigidities in the  people's lives because where people live  you know how long the commute is all  kinds of things how much their house is  worth are all driven by basically public  school districts and you may choose an  area for the good schools for your kids  then the school board waives its magic  wand and your redistricting and now your  kids are in bad schools but you're still  stuck with a long commute and by the way  your property values have dropped twenty  thousand dollars because of the wave of  that magic wand so it's not it's not  optimal and there's no particular reason  why somebody should have to commute an  hour each way just so their kid can have  a decent school that's just how it's  set up for so long there are so many  alternatives now that weren't around a  few years ago and it's not only stuff I  mean you know 20 30 years ago most  communities had a public school system  then there's a private school that was  probably religious usually Catholic and  then maybe there was a Tony or pseudo  Tony private prep school and those were  your choices now there's an online  school there's homeschooling there were  charter schools there's just a wide  variety of other options and it's not  only that the public schools are getting  worse and pushing people out but it's  also that these alternatives that have  sprung up really pulling people in as  well because it's just better talking  about school choice do you see that  option becoming more politically viable  I think sometimes people think it's you  know right-wing Republican thing but  then you have people like Sonia  Sotomayor who is a product of that do  you see a way forward school choice is  super popular in the inner city and that  makes sense when you look at the schools  in the inner city and I think you know  there are a few Republicans who are  beginning to see it as a wedge issue in  the inner city and maybe it is but the  bottom line is school choice is  something everybody wants really and  there are as I say many more things to  choose from I think a lot of big city  schools are basically imploding right  now because parents are banding them  they're literally closing schools and  laying off teachers because they've lost  so many people and that's really  extraordinary when you think about it  because from the standpoint of an  individual parent at least public school  is basically free and people are willing  to surrender a free good and spend their  own money and if their homeschooling  also a lot of their own time educating  their kids somewhere else just because  the free product is so lousy and I think  that the options that people have now  make it much easier to do that and the  thing that will save the public schools  if they're smart enough which remains  open to question is charter schools and  other things that let people try new  stuff but still under the umbrella of  the public school system instead of  exiting completely when I was reading  this book and you know talking about  technology saving education it sounds  great but then you see stories like here  in California where they spent a billion  dollars on iPads it seems like the  schools can't use the technology to save  themselves or even if they try it in the  book I explicitly did  claim that I'm recommending what I call  dumb solutions like give every kid an  iPad there's nothing magic about the  technology you know a kid in a lousy  school with an iPad is just a kid in a  lousy school who now has an iPad what  the technology lets you do though is it  lets you try other approaches so for  example you know the Khan Academy is  famous for its flipped classroom model  where you know in the traditional  classroom the teacher stands up front  and lectures the kids take problems home  and try to work them on their own which  is not so great because their parents  usually don't know the answers under the  car model they flip it the kids watch  the lectures on videos at home then they  come into the classroom and they work  the problems when the teachers right  there to help and explain things well  it's really not the technology that's  doing the work there it's just making  that possible yeah we see people  becoming empowered under this movement  and in 2006 you wrote your book an army  of David's and you were very optimistic  about how you know future and technology  would really empower people to make  changes are you still feeling an  optimistic about that oh yeah pretty  much I mean I have to say you know it's  hard to believe it's been long since I  wrote that book but most of that has  been born out and you certainly see it  in the education area where there is  just a whole lot of ferment I mean  there's more change and excitement going  on in both k12 and higher education now  than there has been for a hundred years  and a lot of stuff that people try is  not going to work but what's left will  be something that's new and better yeah  and you know you started it's dependent  you know right after 9/11 you hear the  blog father like to call you and do you  still think the blogosphere is kind of  this force for cultural change or do you  think that it's too becoming co-opted  and kind of losing its edge of it well  it depends I mean you know the it's  blogging I wrote when blogging was new  and exciting and now it's it's old  school it's so old school that like in  the in the condo we're renting when we  hear that way there's a copy of German  glamour and it has a German article on  lifestyle bloggers and and some of them  are called traditional lifestyle  bloggers and I was laughing because  everything about both the Germans think  it's traditional you know it must be old  but if they're still there I mean the  truth is there were more people reading  blogs now and writing them than there  were when people thought they were  exciting and new 1012 years ago and of  course it's  and other stuff I mean blogs were just  one of the earliest forms of social  media but you have Twitter you have  Facebook you have everything that's  growing up and there'll be new stuff  next year that we're not even talking  about now the important thing is that  it's people talking directly to other  people without gatekeepers and I think  that that's a good trend and I don't see  it going away though I think a lot of  powers-that-be wish it would recently  over the last few years or so you've  become a little bit more outspoken  politically and you know we're heading  into these midterms we've got you know  everyone's already talking about 2016 do  you really think it matters who's in  power or do we kind of see now that  everything's kind of the same and it's  kind of just this Kabuki theater going  on I think it matters who's in power I  think that it's true that depending on  which parties in power you get different  flavors of lousy nasai have flavors I  like that no there's in flavors I don't  like as much so I think I think it does  matter I think that longer term the  question is going to be really how long  the two party is going to be able to run  the Kabuki theater and do their best to  avoid what people really want because  the truth is the public has a set of  priorities which and all you have to  even look at the polls from Gallup or  pew to see the public parties and what  Washington considers priorities are very  different and well I like to say  something that can't go on forever I  don't think that can go on forever  either so I think there'll be some kind  of a realignment that's going to bring  those things closer together  speaking of realignment because we see  more people declaring as independence  what do you make about that neither the  parties that really cover themselves  with glory and neither one is even in a  position to run very effectively on me  well we may not be great but we're  better than the other guys theory  anymore because that that sort of been  running to the ground as well it's hard  for me to know when people call  themselves independents sometimes that  just means I don't want to take  responsibility for my views or I'm  afraid you'll think less of me if I tell  you what I really think but I do think  that you know the traditional parties  used to be a coalition of interest that  really sort of got something out of  being in a coalition together and now  I'm not convinced that their breakup  value  isn't more than their value is going  concerns so many people look to you as a  hero you're a huge figure on the  internet who is your hair who's Glenn  Reynolds Tara's they're an entreprenuer  an innovator or someone the legal field  that you really look up to oh that's  interesting  um my favorite writer in the entire  world as I've said in my blog is Arthur  Allen laughs who was a law professor at  Yale who died right before I got there  but he was very big on touching people's  balloons and my favorite thing that he  wrote was a piece in the Stanford Law  Review called memorandum for from the  devil and it was because Roberto Unger  who was a professor at Harvard had  written a rather pretentious book called  knowledge and politics and the last line  in the book is speak God and so left  responded in the form of a memorandum  from the devil saying that when  non-ironic divine address comes from  Lionel Hall even such as I must take  notice and it was just a brutal takedown  of the moral pretensions of academia so  I guess that's my big hero and I  encourage people look at memorandum from  the devil by Arthur Allen laughs it's  it's short and some of the best writing  you'll read anywhere well go ahead and  thank you so much for joining us today  thank you infer reason TV I'm Alexis  Garcia  you
 

